<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jabalpur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jubbulpore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jabalpur is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Nearby Tewar was the site of Tripuri, the capital of the Kalachuris, and later the region formed the heart of the Gond kingdom of Garha. Under British rule, as Jubbulpore, it became a military and administrative centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a>, and today it is one of the largest cities in Madhya Pradesh.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jabalpur.jpg" alt="Jabalpur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0010322_Chausath_Yogini_Temple_and_Gowri_Shankar_Temple,_Bhedaghat_Madhya_Pradesh_054.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Jabalpur lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada River</a> and is the administrative headquarters of the Jabalpur district and division. Its name is traditionally derived from a sage named Jabali who meditated on the river's banks, though another theory takes it from the Arabic jabal, meaning boulders. The city is known for the Marble Rocks at Bhedaghat, a gorge of marble cut by the Narmada below the Dhuandhar Falls. In 1828 the British officer William Henry Sleeman found a dinosaur fossil near Bara Simla Hill, the first such find in Asia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ashokan relics from about 300 BCE at Rupnath, north of the city, indicate Mauryan presence. Later the region came under the Satavahana dynasty and then became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>. The Kalachuri dynasty conquered it in 875. Under the Gond kings of Garha-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandla">Mandla</a> the city was an important centre of power: Madan Shah built a watchtower and small fort at Madan Mahal, and Rani Durgavati built more than 85 ponds. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> invasion in 1742 made the kingdom a tributary, and the Maratha rulers of Sagar annexed it in 1781. The Bhonsle kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> valley until 1818, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> seized it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sitabuldi">Battle of Sitabuldi</a>.</p><p>The British made Jabalpur the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmada-river">Narmada</a> territories, set up a cantonment and made it the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saugor-and-nerbudda-territories">Saugor and Nerbudda Territories</a>, which joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a> in 1861. The Congress session at nearby Tripuri in 1939 re-elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subhas-chandra-bose">Subhas Chandra Bose</a> as president against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>'s candidate, and he resigned in April of that year. After independence in 1947 the Central Provinces and Berar became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Jabalpur is the seat of the Madhya Pradesh High Court and the zonal headquarters of the West Central Railway. Its municipal population was 1,081,677 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and Hindi is the predominant language. Its cantonment is one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and ordnance factories, including the Gun Carriage Factory begun in 1904, make it a centre of defence industry. It is generally accepted that snooker originated here, devised in 1875 by Colonel Neville Chamberlain. The Hanumantal Bada Jain Mandir, a seventeenth-century temple with 22 shrines, is the largest independent Jain temple in India. The name Jubbulpore was replaced by Jabalpur in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jabalpur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gond rulers established a kingdom around Garha, near modern <b>Jabalpur</b>, perhaps in the 14th century; its early history comes from later genealogies.</blockquote><a href="/state/garha-kingdom">Garha Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>…Tripuri, who claimed descent from the legendary Haihaya king Kartavirya Arjuna and from the Kalachuris of Mahishmati, rose in the late 9th century under Kokalla I. He ruled the Chedi country around Tripuri, modern Tewar near <b>Jabalpur</b>, and strengthened his position through marriage alliances with the Rashtrakuta dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalachuris-of-tripuri">Kalachuris of Tripuri</a></li></ul></section>
